          <>
            <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-main); font-weight: 500;">
              Muhsana was born in Philadelphia in 1967. She spent nine years of formal study in the arts at the university
              level, pursuing fine arts studies at a number of reputable institutions such as Parsons School of Design, PA
              Academy of the Fine Arts, La Sorbonne, University of the Arts, and Moore College of Art. She also trained in
              Bronze Casting with Masters of the Art in Côte d'Ivoire and in mosaic arts with Isaiah Zagar, a renowned
              master of mosaic murals in Philadelphia.
            </p>

            <p>
              From early on, Muhsana had been preoccupied by a desire to understand and know more of her African origins.
              This desire was reflected in her 1993 master’s thesis entitled: <em>The Effects of European Centered
                Education on African American Artists</em>. The question led her to travel on her own to the African
              continent in 1997. That same year, she was provided funding for her project to research traditional and
              contemporary African Art and to produce an exhibition based on that research.
            </p>

            <p>
              After six months of research and travel in Côte d'Ivoire, Mali, Ghana, Burkina Faso, Togo, Senegal and South
              Africa, she later settled in Côte d'Ivoire for the production of her exhibition. Muhsana discovered an
              abandoned building in the center of the business district of the capital city of Abidjan. Her fascination
              with exploring the vastness of that space led to what evolved into a two-year project involving the gang of
              over 50 homeless youth she found living there.
            </p>

            <p>
              This group of wayward teens, many of them drug-addicted and prone to crime and violence, subsequently became
              an integral part of her work. This experience led her to discover the power of art to inspire community
              innovation and to transform human consciousness. This Côte d’Ivoire exhibition, <strong>Portes et Passages
                du Retour (Doors and Passageways of Return)</strong>, became the title of the association she later
              co-founded when she eventually relocated to Senegal.
            </p>

            <p>
              Thus the association Portes et Passages du Retour, and its Center for Art and Holistic Development, situated
              in Mbodiene, Senegal, has served as a vital community hub for that rural region since 2007. The objective of
              this project has been to generate community-centered programs revolving around holistic and alternative
              engagement in all areas of development in the society and a commitment to raising the awareness of creative
              values, improving the manner in which such values are manifested in the everyday lives of inhabitants
              throughout the region.
            </p>

            <p>
              The association has worked with a number of educational and arts institutions throughout the world including
              the Gwangju Biennale; Arts Collaboratory; the Dakar Biennale in Senegal; CIEE international exchange
              program; University of Michigan (to name a few); it has received funding from a number of sources including
              Doen Foundation, and the American Institute of Architects. Muhsana has initiated a number of socially
              interactive works in a number of communities in the U.S. and in Africa. Her work was featured in the
              award-winning film <em>“Philadelphia”</em> and is on display in the White House commemorating the 2001
              bronze award she produced for President Clinton in 2001.
            </p>

            <p>
              Muhsana has participated in numerous international residencies, conferences and lectures around the world.
              She has served as artist in residence and visiting instructor at the University of Southern Maine, and the
              University of Pennsylvania. Muhsana was awarded official selection at the Dakar Biennale. She holds numerous
              awards and honors including a Pew Fellowship in the Arts and a Fulbright fellowship.
            </p>

            <p>
              Muhsana’s work is currently featured in the renovated Africa Galleries at the Penn Museum in Philadelphia,
              PA.
            </p>
          </>
        )}
